A||||15||Fiero_24||Cannot get the 2 front cradle bolts out||||8||
Z||000000||Fiero_24||10-22-2001||12:39 AM||||Well after 1 1/2 days I do not have the old motor out.  I have everything taken apart, but the front 2 cradle bolts are frozen in place.  I have used hammers and pry bars.  I even hit the back side with a mini sledge as someone tried to turn the bolt, and nothing.  I can see the rubber bushing moving, so I can only assume that the inside is stuck to the bolt.  Any suggestions on how to get it out?<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/driverside41.jpg"> <BR>  <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||8
Z||000001||BlackStang95||10-22-2001||12:46 AM||||is it that the bolts arent turning or are they turning but wont come out<P>i suggest getting a craftsman socket and wrench on there good, and getting a really long pipe, putting it over the wrenchand pulling away<P>hitting the ratchet with a hammer usually works too<P>if all else fails youll have to cut the bolts||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fiero_24||10-22-2001||01:03 AM||||I cracked a Craftsman deep well socket down the middle trying to get the darn bolts off.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/driverside41.jpg"> <BR>  <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||8
Z||000003||Oreif||10-22-2001||01:32 AM||||Drill at an angle thru just the rubber bushing to get to the sleeve by the bolt head side. Then flood it with the PB Buster stuff. You may need to spray a few times and let it sit for an hour or so. This should unfreeze the sleeve from the bolt. You could also spray into it from the side you took the nut off. It will take time but it will come loose.<P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">          <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fiero_24||10-22-2001||08:36 AM||||Thanks Oreif.  I am just a little ticked at my car right now.  I just gave up from all the crap I have gone through with this Fiero.  I may go back this weekend and try again, if not I will wait until the following weekend.  It WILL be at the Nov 17th NIFE meeting to show it off.  I am going to order the Poly cradle bushings this week.||||reg||4
Z||000005||TooManyFieros||10-22-2001||12:19 PM||||Orief is correct. Spray it everyday until you are ready to take it out. You can't over-do it. This is one of the worst parts of engine removal. I did quite a few and none have beaten me yet. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||10-22-2001||08:08 PM||||there is a seam in the OE bushing's inner  sleeve. hit that seem with a center punch. A slightly dull punch seems to work better than a really sharp one. That will spread it open and help release it. do this on both ends of the sleeve. It may not let it go right away but it will create a crack for penetrating oil to soak into faster.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000007||voyagerspe||10-22-2001||08:10 PM||||You need to get an impact wrench if you dont have a compressor go rent an electric one.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||Fiero_24||10-22-2001||09:05 PM||||There is no way to get an impact wrench up there (I tried).  The coolant pipes are in the way, and there is just enough room to get a wrench up there.  I will try to find that gap to soak with some PB Buster.  Thanks for all the reply's so far.||||reg||12
Z||000009||GT-maniac||10-22-2001||11:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fiero_24:<BR><B>There is no way to get an impact wrench up there (I tried).  The coolant pipes are in the way, and there is just enough room to get a wrench up there.  I will try to find that gap to soak with some PB Buster.  Thanks for all the reply's so far.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You're right you can't get an impact wrench up there. If you can get the nuts off then the really fun part begins. I used an air chisel to push the bolts out. The pounding action of the chisel loosens the rusted crud in the holes. Of course you'll probably mangle the ends of the bolts but I'd put in new bolts anyway. Once I pushed the bolt flush with the bracket I used another bolt to push it the rest of the way out. Still requires large amounts of profanity and sweat to make it work of course.<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero005a.jpg"> <P>85 WS6 GT partly modified<BR>87 GT Loaded resto project||||reg||1
Z||000010||voyagerspe||10-24-2001||09:52 PM||||Im at a loss.  Ive used my impact on all 3 of my fiero's(85,87,88) to get the front cradle bolts out.  I just droped the cradle to change my tranny in my 88 today and had no problems at all using my impact.  maybe i have a smaller impact wrench then you guys.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by voyagerspe (edited 10-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||WKDFIRO||10-26-2001||09:08 PM||||In the past we used to have bolts that had bound up because of the cradle. Putting a jack underneath one side and slowly jacking up may just unbind it. It wasn't unusual to have to do this with each side. <P>Good luck!||||reg||2
Z||000012||Fiero_24||10-27-2001||08:11 AM||||Thanks for all the info.  Maybe I will try every method told here and it might come loose.  I am going back up to WI 11/02/01 to finish it off.  My dad is working on a device that will press it out, kind of like a steering wheel puller, but opposite.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/driverside41.jpg"> <BR>  <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||10
Z||000013||GOLDSE||11-01-2001||07:42 PM||||The only thing I had luck with on mine, was a torch.  Like you I tried just about everything and even tried a sawzall.  Cutting torch was the only thing to get it out "quickly".  I was replacing the bushing with poly anyway so no big deal.  If you don't have access to a torch, keep up with the PB Blaster.  That stuff is great.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Nashco||11-01-2001||08:01 PM||||Oops....misread...removed due to stupidity.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<p>[This message has been edited by Nashco (edited 11-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||avengador1||11-01-2001||08:09 PM||||Use the die grinder, thats the only way I could get the one that stuck out of mine.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
